    <div class="hbadge rv in">Journal · Guide</div>
    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Is a Private Boat Trip a Good Idea for a Family of Five in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>15 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Yes, and five is exactly the number the boat takes, so the whole family fits with nobody else on board. Because the price is per boat, five people on the 2h30 Day Trip pay 100 EUR each, and the swims, paddle boards and bathroom on board make it work for most ages.</p>

      <h2>Does a family of five really fit?</h2><p>Yes, with no room for a sixth. The boat takes up to five guests, and when you book Chifbay the only other person aboard is your skipper. Two parents and three children, or a parent, a grandparent and three teenagers: any mix of five works.</p><p>What does not work is five plus one. A cousin who joins at the last minute, a nanny, a friend of the eldest. Five is the boat's limit and it is not stretched on the pontoon. Got a baby or a toddler among the five? Message us on WhatsApp first and check how they count.</p><p>She is 9 metres long, white, with grey cushions and a dark canopy that throws shade over part of the deck. With five people there is space to spread out, but not so much that a parent loses sight of anyone.</p><h2>What does it cost per person for five?</h2><p>Less than most families expect, because you pay for the boat, not for seats. Here is what each trip works out at when the price is split five ways:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Trip</th><th>Price for the boat</th><th>Each, for five people</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Day Trip, 2h30</td><td>500 EUR</td><td>100 EUR</td></tr><tr><td>Day Trip, 3h</td><td>600 EUR</td><td>120 EUR</td></tr><tr><td>Sunset Trip, 2h</td><td>400 EUR</td><td>80 EUR</td></tr><tr><td>Sunset Trip, 2h30</td><td>500 EUR</td><td>100 EUR</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A six year old costs the same as an adult, because nothing is priced per head. The price already covers food and drinks, the paddle boards, an Insta360 video in 360°, photos from a proper camera and, if the wind and light let it fly, drone footage. If you are weighing this against a big shared catamaran, our guide that weighs <a href="/posts/private-boat-charter-vs-shared-tour-madeira">a private charter against a seat on a shared tour</a> sets out what changes.</p><h2>Which trip suits a family?</h2><p>The <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip with two swim stops</a>, for nearly every family. It leaves at 10:00 or 14:00 and runs west, with Câmara de Lobos and then the wall of Cabo Girão on the right, to Fajã dos Padres, where everyone can swim, jump in and try the paddle boards. A second swim follows off Ribeira Brava, then a fast run home.</p><p>The Sunset Trip has no swimming and runs into the evening. It leaves 1 h 15 before sunset, which in June is around 20:00, so a 2h30 trip ends at about 22:30. That is late for a seven year old. In winter, with a departure near 16:45, it is gentler, but children who came to swim will still find it slow.</p><h2>How does each age get on?</h2><p>Most ages do well, for different reasons. Plan around the youngest.</p><h3>Babies and toddlers</h3><p>Possible, with more planning: a calm morning, shade, and a shorter trip. Naps, sun and packing for the very young are all in our <a href="/posts/boat-trip-in-madeira-with-a-baby-or-toddler">guide for parents of babies and toddlers on a boat</a>. Ask on WhatsApp about life jackets for very small children before booking.</p><h3>Children from about 5 to 12</h3><p>Usually the happiest people on board. The jump in at Fajã dos Padres becomes a game, and kneeling on a paddle board is easier than it looks for a light child. If a child needs a child size life jacket, ask us on WhatsApp before booking so we can tell you what is possible. Swimmers this age should stay close to an adult in the water, because this is open sea, not a pool.</p><h3>Teenagers</h3><p>They tend to want the fast run back, the jumps and the drone shots for their phone. Our page on <a href="/posts/what-can-teenagers-do-in-madeira">what teenagers can do in Madeira</a> covers the rest of the island for them too.</p><h2>What is the water like for children?</h2><p>Deep, clear and cooler than a hotel pool. The sea off Funchal is usually around 18 °C in March and around 23 to 24 °C in September. Children feel the cold first, especially on the run back with wet hair.</p><ul><li>Bring a dry top or a light hoodie for each child, and a towel each.</li><li>Agree before the stop which adult watches which child in the water.</li><li>Let the skipper say where to jump and where to climb back on.</li><li>Put sun cream on again after each swim. The canopy helps, but the water reflects light back up.</li></ul><h2>Is there a toilet, food and somewhere to sit in the shade?</h2><p>Yes to all three. There is a bathroom on board, which is the single thing that most changes a trip with children. Food is served on board, and the drinks include soft drinks as well as local wine, beer and poncha for the adults. The dark canopy gives shade over part of the seats, so a child who has had enough sun can sit out of it.</p><h2>How do you get five people to the pontoon on time?</h2><p>Treat it like a short flight check in. The boat leaves from Marina do Funchal, halfway along the seafront, and you should be there a quarter of an hour early. Your skipper waits for you on the pontoon itself. With three children, that means leaving the hotel earlier than feels necessary, because someone always needs the toilet on the way out.</p><ul><li>Put swimsuits on under clothes before you leave the room.</li><li>Pack one family bag for towels and dry tops, not five small ones.</li><li>Wear flat shoes with some grip for the wooden pontoon and the deck.</li><li>Leave the big camera at home if you like. The photos and the video are part of the trip.</li></ul><p>Our page with the <a href="/practical">meeting point, packing list and cancellation terms</a> covers the rest.</p><h2>When is a private boat not a good idea for a family?</h2><p>It depends on your family. Be honest about these before you book:</p><ul><li><strong>You are six.</strong> Nobody can be squeezed in. Two trips, or a different plan, are the only answers.</li><li><strong>Someone gets badly seasick.</strong> One unhappy child on a small boat sets the mood for everyone. Pick a calm morning, or skip it.</li><li><strong>A child is scared of deep water.</strong> They can stay on board for the swim stops, but if the whole family is looking forward to jumping in, that child may feel left out.</li><li><strong>The sea is rough.</strong> A skipper who calls off a trip for the sea leaves you to pick: another day, or every euro back. Book early in the holiday so there is time to move.</li></ul><p>If none of those fit, a family of five is close to the ideal group for a Chifbay Day Trip: everyone on one boat, one price, and the coast to yourselves.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Do young children count towards the five guests?</h3><p>The limit is five guests on board. Message us on WhatsApp before booking if one of the five is a baby or toddler, and we will confirm.</p><h3>Is there a bathroom on the boat?</h3><p>Yes, there is a bathroom on board on every trip.</p><h3>Do children pay less on a private boat trip?</h3><p>No. The price is for the whole boat, so it is the same whether your five are adults or children.</p><h3>Are child size life jackets available?</h3><p>Ask us on WhatsApp before you book, with the ages and sizes of your children, so we can tell you what is possible.</p><h3>Which trip is better for a family with young swimmers?</h3><p>The Day Trip, because it has two swim stops, paddle boards and a morning or afternoon departure. The Sunset Trip has no swimming.</p>

      <blockquote>One boat, five towels, and not a stranger in sight.</blockquote>
    </article>
